d $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366616 13194139535794 true] [17592186366615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366617 13194139535794 true] [17592186366615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366618 13194139535794 true] [17592186366615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366619 13194139535794 true] [17592186366615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366620 13194139535794 true] [17592186366615 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535794 true] [17592186366615 1041 "selection" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366615 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366619 13194139535794 true] [17592186366615 1110 #uuid "35be2174-3427-5dd8-9d58-5e8811fb2c32" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366616 1034 "1" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535794 true] [17592186366616 1111 #uuid "e61cfc99-111f-5f0f-a4f3-b0cf0ef49716" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366617 1034 "-4" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366617 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535794 true] [17592186366617 1111 #uuid "6f95f533-4021-5306-87e7-0c30edb155c1" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366618 1034 "4" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535794 true] [17592186366618 1111 #uuid "2993db3b-1f8a-5445-a7c3-97ccdfb158ff" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366619 1034 "-1" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535794 true] [17592186366619 1111 #uuid "56439c31-61a7-5245-9ce7-83f18c3171d9" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366620 1034 "-2" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535794 true] [17592186366620 1111 #uuid "1912c796-b407-53e2-88a2-6bc2cd13add7" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366621 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366622 13194139535794 true] [17592186366621 1089 "q-162551" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366621 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535794 true] [17592186366621 1100 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366621 1113 #uuid "1c160107-8805-58b0-9a37-d31f8eeb4e88" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366621 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨3x,\\,4x-2y+2,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(6x-(4x-2y+2)-(y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y+16)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{2}+16y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366623 13194139535794 true] [17592186366622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366624 13194139535794 true] [17592186366622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366625 13194139535794 true] [17592186366622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366626 13194139535794 true] [17592186366622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366627 13194139535794 true] [17592186366622 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535794 true] [17592186366622 1041 "selection" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366622 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366626 13194139535794 true] [17592186366622 1110 #uuid "3fb79bbb-124a-5f13-b63f-e0a26d3e2b92" 13194139535794 true] [17592186366623 1034 "12" 13194139535